<title>Week 11</title>

<introduction>
    <p>
        This is an outline of the topics we covered in Week 11 of the course, from November 9 to November 13.
    </p>
</introduction>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Monday 11/9</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Understand applications of the Mean Value Theorem
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find maximum and minimum values of functions using the derivative.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The Mean Value Theorem</title>

        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            Recall the Mean Value Theorem from last class:
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Recall the Mean Value Theorem from last class: Full theorem statement.
        </p>


        </paragraphs>
    </page>

<page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Consquences of the Mean Value Theorem</title>

            <p>
                There are a lot of useful consequences of the Mean Value Theorem, since it allows us to tie the derivative to function values.
            </p>

            <corollary xml:id="cor-constant-deriv">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is constant on <m>[a,b]</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>

            <corollary xml:id="cor-same-deriv">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be a functions that are continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = g'(x)</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m> be a functions that are continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) = g'(x)</m> on <m>[a,b]</m>, then <m>f(x) = g(x) + C</m> for some constant <m>C</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Increasing and Decreasing Functions</title>
            
            <p>
                The other main application of the Mean Value Theorem is to analyzing increasing and decreasing functions.
            </p>

            <definition xml:id="def-inc-dec-fcns">
                <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that <m>f(x)</m> is
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on an interval <m>(a,b)</m>. We say that <m>f(x)</m> is increasing, decreasing on <m>(a,b)</m>. This is really hard to check.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </definition>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <corollary xml:id="cor-inc-dec-mvt">
            <statement>
                    <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. 
                        If <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, 
                    </p>
                    <p component="a">
                        Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is continuous on <m>[a,b]</m> and differentiable on <m>(a,b)</m>. If <m>f'(x) \gt 0</m> on <m>(a,b)</m>, then <m>f(x)</m> is increasing on <m>(a,b)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </corollary>
    </page>


    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Max and Min Values of Functions</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                The next application we want to analyze is finding the maximum and minimum values of functions.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The next application we want to analyze is finding the maximum and minimum values of functions. Foreshadowing of optimization problems, where we'll want to do this for word problems.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            So, what are we looking for here? We're looking for the biggest and smallest values that <m>f(x)</m> takes on over an interval.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-extreme-values">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>c</m> be some point in <m>[a,b]</m>.
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>c</m> be some point in <m>[a,b]</m>. Absolute maximum, Absolute minimum.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <p component="b" workspace="3in">
            <nbsp />
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Graph that has max, min, locals.
        </p>

        <definition xml:id="def-local-extreme-values">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>c</m> be some point in <m>[a,b]</m>.
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on some interval <m>[a,b]</m> and <m>c</m> be some point in <m>[a,b]</m>. Local maximum, Local minimum.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="3in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the graph of the function given below:
                </p>
                <image width="60%">
                <shortdescription>Graph of a polynomial.</shortdescription>
                <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org"
             label="max-min-poly-one">
            <diagram xmlns="https://prefigure.org" dimensions="(300,300)" margins="5">
  <definition> f(x)=3*x^5-15*x^4-55*x^3 + 225*x^2</definition>
  <coordinates bbox="[-6,-2000,8,2000]">
    <grid-axes xlabel="x" ylabel="y"/>
    <graph function="f"/>
  </coordinates>
</diagram>
            </prefigure>
            </image>
                <p>
                    Identify any absolute and local maximum/minimum values on <m>[-4, 7]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            For simpler examples, we can look at <m>x^2</m> and <m>x^3</m>.
        </p>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            <nbsp />
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-extreme-vales">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="2in">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on <m>[a,b]</m>. If 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function defined on <m>[a,b]</m>. If  continuous, then attains max and min.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="2in">
            That theorem tells us that there will be a maximum and minimum value in specific situations, but it gives us no way to actually find these points.
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            That theorem tells us that there will be a maximum and minimum value in specific situations, but it gives us no way to actually find these points. It turns out that local extreme values are easier to find, and local + endpoints are the only possibilities for the absolute max and min.
        </p>

        <theorem xml:id="thm-fermat-extreme">
            <statement>
                <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                    If <m>f(c)</m> is a local maximum or minimum value for <m>f(x)</m> and 
                </p>
                <p component="a">
                    If <m>f(c)</m> is a local maximum or minimum value for <m>f(x)</m> and  <m>f'(c)</m> exists, then <m>f'(c) = 0</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </theorem>

        <p component="b" workspace="1in">
            <nbsp />
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Define critical points as well. And the process.
        </p>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x) = 2x^3 + 9x^2 - 60x + 8</m> on <m>[-8, 4]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x) = x^{2/3}(10-x)</m> on <m>[-1, 12]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>
</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Tuesday 11/10</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Find absolute maxima and minima of functions on closed intervals using Calculus.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the derivative to determine when a function is increasing and decreasing.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the second derivative to analyze the shape of a graph.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Absolute Maximum and Minimum Values</title>

        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the critical points of the function <m>f(x) = x^2e^x</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            So, what does the full process of solving one of these problems look like?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            So, what does the full process of solving one of these problems look like? Critical points, endpoints, pick which is the biggest and smallest.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x) = 2x^3 +9x^2 - 24x + 5</m> on <m>[-2, 2]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x) = 2 - |x|</m> on <m>[-1, 3]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find the absolute maximum and minimum values of the function <m>f(x) = x^3 - 27x</m> on <m>[-4, 2]</m>.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Derivatives and the Shape of a Graph</title>

            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                Now, we want to use the derivative to help us analyze the shape of a graph. We looked at one part of this last time.
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                Now, we want to use the derivative to help us analyze the shape of a graph. We looked at one part of this last time. Increasing and Decreasing.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Find all intervals where the function <m>f(x) = 2x^3 + 9x^2 - 60x + 8</m> is increasing and decreasing.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Sign Charts</title>
            
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            Our main tool in this process is the sign chart, which you may have seen previously. What is this process?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            Our main tool in this process is the sign chart, which you may have seen previously. What is this process? Zeros and undefined, plug in points to get positive or negative.
        </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
       <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Make the sign chart for the function <md>
                    f(x) = \frac{x^2 - 4x - 5}{x+3}
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
       
       </example>
       
    </page>

    <page>
       <example workspace="4in">
        <statement>
            <p>
                Make the sign chart for the function <md>
                    f(x) = \frac{x-5}{x^2 + 3x + 2}
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </statement>
       
       </example>
       
    </page>

    <page>
        <p>
            If we want to use this to talk about functions increasing or decreasing, we want the sign chart for <m>f'(x)</m>, not for <m>f(x)</m>.
        </p>

        <example workspace="4in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Use a sign chart to determine where the function <m>f(x) = 2x^3 - 15x^2 - 36x + 1</m> is increasing and decreasing.
                </p>
            </statement>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The First Derivative Test</title>
            <p component="b" workspace="3in">
                The first derivative test is a way to determine if a critical point is a local maximum or local minimum without plugging in values. How does our function have to be set up to get a local maximum?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                The first derivative test is a way to determine if a critical point is a local maximum or local minimum without plugging in values. How does our function have to be set up to get a local maximum? Increase before, decrease after. This gives us the test.
            </p>
            
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>The Second Derivative</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                If <m>f'(x)</m> measures the rate of change of the function <m>f(x)</m>, what does <m>f''(x)</m> measure?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                If <m>f'(x)</m> measures the rate of change of the function <m>f(x)</m>, what does <m>f''(x)</m> measure? It's the rate of change of the slopes. If <m>f''(x) \gt 0</m>, then the slopes have to be increasing, which means we're above our tangent lines. This is what we mean by concave up.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Test for Concavity</title>
            
            <p component="b" workspace="4in">
                How do we know if a function is concave up or concave down?
            </p>
            <p component="a">
                How do we know if a function is concave up or concave down? We use the second derivative.
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <p component="b" workspace="4in">
            So, given a function, how can we determine where it is concave up and concave down?
        </p>
        <p component="a">
            So, given a function, how can we determine where it is concave up and concave down? We need a sign chart for <m>f''(x)</m>.
        </p>
    </page>

    <page>
        <example workspace="0in">
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Consider the function <md>
                        f(x) = \frac{x}{x^2 + 1}
                    </md>.
                    
                </p>
            </introduction>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Create a sign chart for <m>f</m>, <m>f'</m>, and <m>f''</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
        
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is increasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is decreasing.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Find and classify any local maxima or minima of <m>f(x)</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave up.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
            <task workspace="2in">
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Determine on which intervals <m>f(x)</m> is concave down.
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </task>
        
        </example>
        
    </page>

</handout>

<handout component="cur">
    <title>Wednesday 11/11</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Review for Exam 2
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Exam 2 Topics</title>

            <p>
                Exam 2 is coming up on Friday, November 13. It covers:
                <ul>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.1: Basic derivative formulas (powers, exponential, sum, difference)
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.2: Product and Quotient Rules
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.3: Derivatives of Trigonometric Functions
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.4: Chain Rule
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.5: Implicit Differentiation
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.6: Derivatives of Inverse Functions (ln, inverse trig, exponential with non-e base) 
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.7: Derivatives in Nature (velocity and motion problems)
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.9: Related Rates
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 3.10: Linear Approximation
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Section 4.4: L'Hospital's Rule
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </p>
            <p>
                You should expect all of the derivative rules to be combined together. The problem will likely say something like <q>Take the derivative of this function</q> and you'll need to know how to handle it from there.
            </p>

        </paragraphs>
    </page>

    <page>
        <paragraphs>
            <title>Exam Review</title>
            <p>
                Where do we want to start?
            </p>
        </paragraphs>
    </page>
</handout>


